P|\psi\rangle = \begin{bmatrix} 0 & 1/\sqrt{2} & 0 \\ 1/\sqrt{2} & 0 & 1/\sqrt{2} \\ 0 & 1/\sqrt{2} & 0 \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ 2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
= \begin{bmatrix} (1/\sqrt{2})(2) \\ (-1 + 1/\sqrt{2}) \\ (1/\sqrt{2})(2) \end{bmatrix}

Now, let's calculate the inner product of |\psi\rangle and P|\psi\rangle:

\langle\psi|P|\psi\rangle = \begin{bmatrix} -1 & 2 & 1 \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} (1/\sqrt{2})(2) \\ (-1 + 1/\sqrt{2}) \\ (1/\sqrt{2})(2) \end{bmatrix}
= (-1)(1/\sqrt{2})(2) + 2(-1 + 1/\sqrt{2}) + 1(1/\sqrt{2})(2)
= -\sqrt{2} - 2 + 2/\sqrt{2} + 2/\sqrt{2}
= -\sqrt{2} + 4/\sqrt{2}

To find the expectation value of Q, we need to calculate the inner product of |\psi\rangle and Q|\psi\rangle.

First, let's calculate Q|\psi\rangle:

Q|\psi\rangle = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & -1 \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ 2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
= \begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ 0 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix}

Now, let's calculate the inner product of |\psi\rangle and Q|\psi\rangle:

